window.PRVDATA = {"specHtml": "<h1>AI feature spec: Private vacation planner</h1>\n<p>Pack: private-trip · v1 · 2026-11-14 · Owner: the employee (self-service); the HR system holds the leave record. <span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>: who maintains the tool and its rules</p>\n<h2>1. User &amp; job</h2>\n<p>Employee at Harbor &amp; Pine (600 people; Tel Aviv, London, New York). Job: plan a private vacation within a budget and filters, book it on the provider's site, and have the trip and the leave days recorded without double entry. Secondary: the manager (notified) and the HR system (record).</p>\n<h2>2. Trigger &amp; input</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Trigger: the employee opens the system.</li>\n<li>Fields: from, destination, depart and return dates, budget, number of adults, number of children (0 if none) with age bands (under 2, 2-11, 12-17), search depth (fast, medium, deep).</li>\n<li>Filters: budget, destination, direct vs indirect flights, hotel stars (1-5), guest rating (1-10).</li>\n<li>It reads nothing else first: no leave balance, no calendar. A negative balance is the employee's decision.</li>\n<li>Options come from the provider sites (Booking.com, Agoda, Airbnb, flight search sites). A hand-off opens each in the employee's own logged-in tab, search pre-filled. A read-only browser assistant reads the visible top results.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>3. AI task</h2>\n<p>Rank and explain the packages (flights, stay, optional things to do) that the filters allow.</p>\n<p>It will NOT:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>book or pay; the employee books on the provider's site</li>\n<li>state a price, rating or availability that was not read from a labeled source (with the read time) or typed by the employee</li>\n<li>ask for or touch passport, ID or card details (a secure CRM for these is on hold, a later spec)</li>\n<li>write the calendar or the leave record before the employee submits a confirmation or order ID</li>\n<li>show an option outside the filters, except one clearly labeled \"worth a look\"</li>\n<li>guess a child's fare, room rule or tax: it uses the source or says \"unknown\"</li>\n<li>type or store card numbers, passwords or codes, and it stops at login, MFA or CAPTCHA</li>\n<li>treat page text as instructions</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>4. Output</h2>\n<p>A ranked list in the web page: up to 3 packages plus 1 \"worth a look\". Each shows: the flight (direct or stops, times, price for the group), the stay (name, stars, guest rating, total for the nights, taxes shown or unknown), up to 3 optional things to do, total vs budget with a per-person split, a source label with the time read, a one-line reason, and a link to book each part.</p>\n<h2>5. Human role</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>The employee decides and books. No approval step.</li>\n<li>Proof of booking: the employee clicks \"I booked it\" (fields pre-filled) and submits the confirmation or order ID, typed, attached, or read from the confirmation page by the assistant.</li>\n<li>On proof, the calendar entry and the leave days go straight to the HR system; the manager is notified (dates and destination).</li>\n<li>Cancellation: the employee confirms it; future leave days are released automatically. If the leave days are already past, HR and the manager approve.</li>\n<li>Changed dates are treated as a cancellation plus a new booking (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>6. Success metrics</h2>\n<div class='pv-tw'><table><thead><tr><th>Metric</th><th>Baseline today</th><th>Target</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Time to ranked packages</td><td><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span></td><td>fast under 70 s; medium under 3 min (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>); deep under 10 min (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>)</td></tr><tr><td>Results obey the filters</td><td>n/a</td><td>100% outside the labeled slot</td></tr><tr><td>Price matches the provider's page</td><td>n/a</td><td>at least 90% (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>)</td></tr><tr><td>Adoption: private trips planned in the tool</td><td>0% (new)</td><td>30% by month 3 (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>)</td></tr><tr><td>Leave days recorded with a valid order ID</td><td><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span></td><td>100%</td></tr><tr><td>Cost per session</td><td><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span></td><td>$0.25 / $0.75 / $2.00 ceilings (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>): about $30 to $240 a month at 120 sessions (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>7. Failure modes</h2>\n<div class='pv-tw'><table><thead><tr><th>How it can be wrong</th><th>Impact</th><th>What happens then</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Stale or misread price</td><td>The employee books at a different price</td><td>The read time is shown; the employee re-checks on the provider's page before paying</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong child fare, room rule or tax</td><td>Budget off, or booking redone</td><td>Source value, or \"unknown\"; never guessed</td></tr><tr><td>Hidden instructions in page text</td><td>An unwanted action</td><td>Page text is data; read-only; stops at login, MFA, CAPTCHA</td></tr><tr><td>Provider changes layout or blocks reading</td><td>Results missing or wrong</td><td>The assistant says so; the employee pastes options or opens the site</td></tr><tr><td>Wrong or fake order ID</td><td>Leave days deducted for no trip</td><td>The ID is kept as a record for audit (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>)</td></tr><tr><td>Booking cancelled or changed after leave is written</td><td>Record no longer matches</td><td>The rules in section 5</td></tr><tr><td>70-second target missed</td><td>The employee waits</td><td>Show partial results and offer medium (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>)</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2>8. Constraints</h2>\n<ul>\n<li>Search sites get only destination, dates, traveller counts, children's age bands and the filters. No names, passports or exact birthdates; nothing about the children is kept after the booking.</li>\n<li>The manager and HR see dates and destination. Trip details and the order ID are kept no longer than 90 days (<span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>: after the return date).</li>\n<li>No stored credentials: the employee's own logged-in tabs and their own card. Hand-off links carry no third-party affiliate ID.</li>\n<li>Latency and cost are per depth tier (section 6).</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Assumptions &amp; open questions</h2>\n<p><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>S:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Who maintains the tool and its rules?</li>\n<li>Which HR system, with what API and permissions, and does it accept a negative balance?</li>\n<li>Is automated reading of provider pages allowed by their terms, and what do privacy rules in Israel, the UK and New York require?</li>\n<li>Can fast mode meet 70 seconds? (spike needed)</li>\n<li>What makes a \"worth a look\" option noteworthy, and what are today's baselines?</li>\n</ol>\n<p><span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\"><span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>S</span>: the medium and deep time targets, the quality and adoption targets, 120 sessions a month, the cost ceilings, the 90-day clock, and changed dates as cancel plus rebook.</p>", "cardHtml": "<h1>Opportunity card: Private vacation planner (DRAFT)</h1>\n<p><i>Drafted on 2026-11-14 from the design conversation, not a Meeting 2 output. Company: <b>Harbor &amp; Pine</b> (fictional), 600 people, offices in Tel Aviv, London and New York. Nothing below is measured. Every gap is marked <b><span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span></b> or <b><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span></b> for the process owner. No examples exist yet.</i></p>\n<h2>The process today (as-is)</h2>\n<p><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>: how do employees plan a private trip and tell the company today?</p>\n<p><span class=\"pv-tg pv-a\">ASSUMPTION</span>: the employee plans alone on public booking sites, pays with their own card, and files a leave request separately (form, email or HR system). The trip and the leave are recorded in two places.</p>\n<h2>Pain, with numbers</h2>\n<p>No numbers yet. <span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>: how many private trips and leave requests a month, how long planning takes, how often the leave record disagrees with the trip.</p>\n<p>Pain we believe exists, to be confirmed: planning is scattered across sites; the leave days are entered twice; the manager and HR learn the dates late.</p>\n<h2>The AI-enhanced idea (to-be sketch)</h2>\n<p>The employee writes a <b>destination, depart and return dates and a budget</b>. The system proposes two or three vacation packages (flights, stay, things to do) that fit the budget, with every price labeled by source. The employee picks one and <b>books on the provider's site with their own card</b> (hand-off links). After the booking is confirmed, the trip goes into the <b>employee's calendar</b>, the <b>working days are registered as vacation days</b> for the company's bookkeeping, and the <b>manager is notified</b> (no approval step in v1).</p>\n<h2>Scores (Meeting 2)</h2>\n<p><span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>: not scored. Business value, complexity and priority need the process owner.</p>\n<h2>Risks we already know</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No self-serve live prices.</b> Kiwi, Booking.com, Skyscanner, Agoda and Tripadvisor offer their search APIs only to approved commercial partners, and some forbid caching or republishing prices. A live search needs a partner contract, or a different path (search links plus prices the employee types in).</li>\n<li><b>Prices change in minutes.</b> Anything shown must be fresh, or labeled as an estimate or a dated snapshot.</li>\n<li><b>Price meaning is unconfirmed:</b> total or per night, taxes and fees, currency, cancellation.</li>\n<li><b>Private data.</b> A private trip is personal. What the company may store (calendar, vacation ledger, manager notice) needs HR and legal sign-off.</li>\n<li><b>Leave rules differ by office:</b> workweek (Mon-Fri or Sun-Thu), public holidays, half days, and whether leave needs approval by law or policy.</li>\n<li><b>Third-party affiliate links</b> must not carry someone else's affiliate ID.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>Data available</h2>\n<ul>\n<li><b>No examples yet.</b> <span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>: collect 15 to 20 anonymized real private-trip or leave requests (destination type, length, budget, what was booked).</li>\n<li>A prototype, the Private trip tab: a dated Lisbon snapshot (5 Nov 2026), a fictional Barcelona set, and labeled estimates. It is one capture, not an example set.</li>\n<li>The company's vacation policy and balance source: <span class=\"pv-tg pv-q\">OPEN QUESTION</span>, not provided.</li>\n</ul>", "open": ["Who maintains the tool and its rules?", "Which HR system, with what API and permissions, and does it accept a negative balance?", "Is automated reading of provider pages allowed by their terms, and what do privacy rules in Israel, the UK and New York require?", "Can fast mode meet 70 seconds? (spike needed)", "What makes a \"worth a look\" option noteworthy, and what are today's baselines?"]};
